<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivar of Limerick</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/977">977</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> was the Norse king of Limerick in the later 10th century. He allied with Irish kings of Munster against the rising Dál Cais, but Mathgamain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> defeated the Norse at Sulcoit in 967 and sacked Limerick. Ivar was implicated in Mathgamain's killing in 976 and was killed by Brian in 977.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivar's father is not named in the Irish annals, but he is generally accepted as a member of the Uí Ímair, the Norse dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imar">Ímar</a> (died 873). The Cogad Gáedel re Gallaib calls him Ímar ua Ímair, grandson of Ímar, which may be a surname rather than a literal descent, since a grandson of the first Ímar would have been very old by 977. How he was related to Aralt mac Sitric, the last earlier known king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a>, who died in 940, is uncertain.</p><p>Most of what is known comes from the Cogad, an early 12th-century saga written to glorify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brian-boru">Brian Boru</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-cais">Dál gCais</a> for his descendant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muirchertach-ua-briain">Muirchertach Ua Briain</a>, and from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-inisfallen">Annals of Inisfallen</a>, which have no entries from mid-969 to 972. The Cogad has Ivar arrive with a great fleet and his sons Dubcenn, Cú Allaidh and Aralt, camp at Inis Sibtonn in the harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limerick">Limerick</a> and impose kings, reeves and tribute on Munster, a claim long doubted by scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 969 the annals record that Ivar killed Beólán Litil and his son, probably the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ui-neill">Uí Néill</a> king of Southern Brega, which places the Limerick Norse active near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. In 972 the nobles of Munster, among them Mathgamain and the son of Bran, resolved to banish the Norse soldiers from Munster and the foreigners from Limerick and to burn the fortress. In 974 Maccus mac Arailt, King of Mann and the Isles, plundered Inis Cathaig and carried Ivar off as a captive; in 975 Ivar escaped over sea, or by other readings was released, perhaps for a ransom.</p><p>According to the Cogad, in 976 Ivar induced his ally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnuban-mac-cathail">Donnubán mac Cathail</a> to receive Mathgamain in his house and betray him to Máel Muad mac Brain, who had him killed. The annals record the betrayal and the killing but do not mention Ivar's part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 977 Brian killed Ivar and two of his sons, Amlaíb Cú Allaidh and Dubcenn, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scattery-island">Scattery Island</a>, apparently taking them by surprise, possibly in revenge for Mathgamain. Their deaths ended an independent Norse Limerick, fifty-five years after the arrival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomrair-mac-ailchi">Tomrair mac Ailchi</a> in 922. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-inisfallen">Annals of Inisfallen</a> give Ivar the style rí Gall, King of the Foreigners, otherwise used for the kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odonovan-family">O'Donovan family</a> claims descent from him through a daughter said to have married Donnubán.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivar of Limerick” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 960s <b>Ivar of Limerick</b> allied with Irish kings of Munster against the rising Dal Cais.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-limerick">Kingdom of Limerick</a></li><li><blockquote>Its king Donnubán mac Cathail allied with <b>Ivar of Limerick</b> and helped bring about the death of Mathgamain, brother of Brian Bóruma, in 976, which drew Brian's revenge.</blockquote><a href="/state/ui-fidgenti">Ui Fidgenti</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Inis Cathaigh, in what is now <a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.61,-9.52&amp;year=967" aria-label="Inis Cathaigh on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4337 349 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Ivar of Limerick"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4787 619h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4787 619h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=52.61,-9.52&amp;year=967">What was here in 967 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
